<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chakradhar Singh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1905">1905</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chakradhar Singh was the Raja of Raigarh, a princely state in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, from 1924. A musician and dancer himself, he gathered leading Kathak dancers, singers and instrumentalists at his court and compiled treatises on dance and rhythm, giving rise to the Raigarh gharana of Kathak. The annual Chakradhar Samaroh festival of music and dance in Raigarh commemorates him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chakradhar-singh.jpg" alt="Chakradhar Singh" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Monuraj124 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raja_Bahadur_Chakradhar_Singh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chakradhar Singh was born on 19 August 1905, a son of Raja Bhup Deo Singh, who died in 1917, and belonged to the Raj Gond dynasty that ruled Raigarh and held the chiefship of Bargarh. He was educated at Rajkumar College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raipur">Raipur</a>. He succeeded his elder brother, Raja Natwar Singh, on his death in 1924, but government management of the state continued until 1927, when the raja was invested with ruling powers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Experts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a> gharanas, and some of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Benares</a> gharana, held prominent places at his court; among the Kathak masters who served there were Pandit Jaggannath Prasad of the Jaipur gharana and Guru Kalaka Prasad and his sons of the Lucknow gharana. By mixing different forms he shaped a new style, the Raigarh gharana, the youngest of the gharanas, with its own thumris, ghazals, todas and bols. He was himself a player of tabla and pakhawaj, and his name is often linked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wajid-ali-shah">Wajid Ali Shah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> as a patron of Kathak and Hindustani classical music.</p><p>He knew Hindi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odia-language">Oriya</a> and wrote several books on music, among them Nartan Sarwasya, Raag Ratna Manjusha and Taal Toynidhi; Sanskrit scholars at his court, including Pandit Sadashiv Das and Pandit Kashi Dutt Jha, helped him translate music and dance literature in Sanskrit. In 1938 he headed the All India Music Conference at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a>, attending with a team of 60 artists, and at later gatherings he played tabla for Kathak performances. The Viceroy, Lord Linlithgow, gave him the title Sangit Samrat, King of Music.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died on 7 October 1947, just after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a>. His son Lalit Kumar Singh succeeded him and ruled briefly before Raigarh State was merged into the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> on 14 December 1947; Raigarh, Jashpur, Sakti, Sarangarh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> were later united to form Raigarh district. His second son, Surendra Kumar Singh, became a politician of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>, and a music academy at Raigarh was established as his memorial.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chakradhar Singh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Raja <b>Chakradhar Singh</b>, who reigned from 1924, gathered leading musicians and dancers at his court and wrote treatises on dance, giving rise to the Raigarh gharana of Kathak.</blockquote><a href="/state/raigarh-state">Raigarh State</a></li></ul></section>
